Title:
ETW-TI Bypass (Disabling ETW Threat Intelligence via DKOM)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up reverse engineers Windows ETW for Threat Intelligence (ETW-TI), a kernel-only telemetry channel heavily used by EDRs to detect suspicious activity like remote memory allocation and process injection. It traces how executable memory allocations (e.g., via `VirtualAllocEx` → `NtAllocateVirtualMemory` → `MiAllocateVirtualMemory`) trigger ETW-TI logging through `EtwTiLogAllocExecVm`. The core finding is that many ETW-TI events are gated by `EtwProviderEnabled()`, which relies on mutable kernel data structures (`_ETW_REG_ENTRY` → `_ETW_GUID_ENTRY` → `_TRACE_ENABLE_INFO`). By performing a data-only kernel attack (DKOM) and flipping `ProviderEnableInfo.IsEnabled` (and related group enable state) to `0`, the PoC causes `EtwpLevelKeywordEnabled()` to early-exit and drop ETW-TI events. The author demonstrates that after the modification, Elastic Defend no longer observes key virtual memory telemetry (e.g., ALLOCVM/WRITEVM events) during injection. This is primarily useful for kernel-capable red teams/malware developers and for defenders to understand a high-impact telemetry blinding primitive.

Technical Focus:
- ETW vs ETW-TI architecture and privilege separation (kernel-only TI channel)
- Windows kernel call chain for memory allocation telemetry (`MiAllocateVirtualMemory` → `EtwTiLogAllocExecVm`)
- Reversing and patchless “data-only” bypass via DKOM
- ETW provider gating logic (`EtwProviderEnabled`, `EtwpLevelKeywordEnabled`)
- Undocumented kernel structures: `_ETW_REG_ENTRY`, `_ETW_GUID_ENTRY`, `_TRACE_ENABLE_INFO`
- PPL/ELAM constraints around ETW-TI consumers (context for why ETW-TI exists)

Use Cases:
- Blinding EDR telemetry that depends on ETW-TI for process injection / virtual memory activity
- Researching kernel telemetry pipelines and ETW-TI event generation points
- Building defensive detections for ETW-TI tampering / provider state manipulation
- Validating EDR resilience when ETW-TI is unavailable or corrupted

Keywords:
ETW, ETW-TI, Threat Intelligence, Windows kernel, DKOM, EtwProviderEnabled, EtwpLevelKeywordEnabled, EtwThreatIntProvRegHandle, _ETW_REG_ENTRY, _ETW_GUID_ENTRY, _TRACE_ENABLE_INFO, ProviderEnableInfo, IsEnabled, MiAllocateVirtualMemory, NtAllocateVirtualMemory, VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory, process injection, PPL, ELAM, EDR telemetry, Windows 11 25H2, IDA Pro, WinDbg